AC4 investigation — NEARS-2721 QA (2026-09-01)

GOAL: grep Admin/storage/logs/laravel.log by AC1's successful item-details
response X-Request-Id, confirm no ModuleCheckMiddleware 403 rejection line,
paired with a POSITIVE CONTROL confirming the same grep CAN find such a line
for a deliberately-broken repro (so the absence check isn't vacuous).

STEP 1 — AC1 success case (item 51, store 12 "Fresh local", zone 2, module 1):
Flutter app log:
  14:46:22.456 [NET] GET endpoint=/api/v1/items/details/51
  14:46:23.715 [NET] endpoint=/api/v1/items/details/51 http_status=200
  (no [FAIL] line for this endpoint — success, no correlationId logged by
  design since AppLogger.failure is only called on error paths)
Backend laravel.log grepped for the exact timestamp window
  `2026-09-01 14:4[4-8]` — zero lines mention items/details, get_product,
  or ModuleCheckMiddleware. Only unrelated pre-existing OTel
  circuit-breaker noise (localhost:5080 collector not running) appears.
  => absence confirmed (trivially).

STEP 2 — POSITIVE CONTROL (deliberately broken repro):
  $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w "HTTP %{http_code}\n" \
      "http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1/items/details/51" -H "zoneId: [2]"
  HTTP 403                      <- confirms ModuleCheckMiddleware DID reject
                                    (no moduleId header sent)
  laravel.log line count before: 188807, after: 188813 (+6 lines)
  All 6 new lines are unrelated background OTel circuit-breaker export
  failures (queue/reverb process), NOT related to this curl call at all.
  => ZERO log lines were written for the 403 rejection itself.

ROOT CAUSE: Admin/app/Http/Middleware/ModuleCheckMiddleware.php returns
`response()->json([...], 403)` directly with NO Log::/AppLogger call
anywhere in the class. Admin/app/Http/Controllers/Api/V1/ItemController.php
get_product()'s own separate out_of_zone 403 branch (NEARS-1024, line ~625)
is likewise a bare `return response()->json(...)` with no logging.

CONCLUSION: The positive control FAILED to produce a matching log line —
this means AC4's literal grep-for-absence method is UNFALSIFIABLE in this
codebase: the same "no line found" result occurs whether the bug is present
or fixed, because the 403 rejection path (both ModuleCheckMiddleware's
blanket check and get_product's own zone check) never writes to
laravel.log at all, regardless of outcome. AC4 is marked `unverifiable` by
its literal BE-log method for this reason (not `met:false` — the underlying
behavior is independently confirmed correct via the FE-side evidence below).

ALTERNATE EVIDENCE (functionally equivalent, gathered instead):
- FE [NET]/[FAIL] logs: AC1's request returned http_status=200, no [FAIL]
  line at all (a real ModuleCheckMiddleware rejection would show
  http_status=403 with a paired [FAIL] line, as demonstrated live in AC2
  correlation_id=bb78310e-7e4d-407b-aecf-b3cccb5d24e3, http_status=403,
  which — being a genuine out-of-zone item, not a missing-header case —
  is get_product's own out_of_zone check, not ModuleCheckMiddleware; body
  distinguishable by error code `out_of_zone` vs `moduleId`).
- Flutter route log shows `route: module match found=true for slug=1`
  BEFORE the item-details fetch fires (i.e. `checkModuleId`/`setModule`
  completed and the moduleId header was populated), confirming the actual
  fix mechanism (module resolved before fetch) executed as designed.

RECOMMENDATION (followup, non-blocking): file a small backend observability
ticket to add a structured Log::warning/AppLogger-equivalent failure line to
ModuleCheckMiddleware's 403 branch (and/or get_product's out_of_zone/404
branches) per the logging-contract's "every failure path is logged" mandate
— today NEITHER branch logs anything, making any future BE-log-based
verification of this middleware equally vacuous.
